{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q022_colg_crya_remote_homology","answer":"In SCOPe 2.04, the ColG collagen-binding domain from PDB 1nqd and domain III, the C-terminal domain, of Cry3A from PDB 1dlc were classified in separate folds and therefore had no asserted evolutionary relationship. Current SCOPe instead places them in one superfamily while retaining them as separate families. The revision therefore represents recognition of remote common ancestry at the superfamily level, not close family-level homology.

The evidence is more specific than both domains merely being all-beta proteins: their structures share a detailed jelly-roll/beta-sandwich core, corresponding strand connectivity and packing, and alignable core residues despite little readily detectable sequence identity. Their biological roles are also compatible with divergence from an ancestral targeting module. The ColG domain recruits collagenase to collagen, whereas Cry3A domain III contributes to recognition of insect-midgut receptor or glycan determinants, toxin specificity, and structural stability. Thus both are terminal ligand-recognition domains attached to larger extracellular bacterial effector proteins.

This classification does not justify transferring ligand specificity or whole-protein function: Cry3A should not be annotated as collagen-binding, and ColG should not be assigned insecticidal receptor binding or toxicity. Cry3A can serve only as a remote structural template for the conserved core of ColG, with loops and binding surfaces treated cautiously. Representatives should remain family-specific. Functional transfer would require external structure-guided sequence/profile evidence plus direct biochemical ligand- or receptor-binding data.","evidence_used":["pretrained model knowledge"]}]}
